# Retirement ledger — one line per task removed from tasks.jsonl, ever.
#
# A defect task is data ABOUT source. When that source is deleted the task
# cannot be re-authored against anything, and leaving it in the corpus makes
# every run fail to apply it. Removing it is therefore legitimate — but only
# when it is RECORDED, because the alternative is indistinguishable from
# deleting a task the tree merely got worse at.
#
# `split` is the split the task occupied when it was retired, so the dev/sealed
# accounting stays reconstructable from this file alone; unit tests re-derive it
# from the id and the committed SEAL_SALT and refuse a line that misreports it.
# Retiring never re-rolls anyone else's split: membership is a pure function of
# the id, so the remaining tasks keep the assignment they always had.
#
# Retirement is a last resort. A defect class that still exists in relocated or
# renamed code is RE-AUTHORED against it instead — see the same-dated commit
# that moved stat-line-size-and-mtime-swapped onto execution/exec-result.ts and
# device-mount-consent-prefix-escape onto execution/device-tunnel-executor.ts,
# both of which looked equally dead from the patch alone.
#
# A line is WITHDRAWN from this file when a later reading finds the class alive
# somewhere else and re-authors the task against it: the id is back in
# tasks.jsonl, where a retired id may not be, and the accounting hole the line
# existed to explain is closed by the task's return rather than by the record.
# think-caller-tuning-clobbered was withdrawn that way — retired when the fork
# cutover deleted the deep-merge it inverted, re-authored onto the evolution
# engine's constructor, where injected defaults spread over a caller's explicit
# config is the same precedence and three tests already refuse the inversion.
